{"bill":{"id":"119-sjres-193","congress":119,"billType":"sjres","billNumber":"193","title":"A joint resolution to direct the removal of United States Armed Forces from hostilities within or against the Republic of Cuba that have not been authorized by Congress.","introducedDate":"2026-05-20","latestActionDate":"2026-05-20","latestActionText":"Read twice and referred to the Committee on Foreign Relations.","policyArea":"International Affairs","subjects":[],"sponsorBioguideId":"K000384","cosponsorBioguideIds":["S001150","G000574"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"This joint resolution directs the President to remove U.S. Armed Forces from hostilities within or against Cuba unless a declaration of war or authorization to use military force for such purpose has been enacted. The resolution specifies that it shall not be construed to prevent the United States from defending itself from an armed attack, the threat of an imminent armed attack, or the lawful execution of counternarcotics operations.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/senate-joint-resolution/193","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/sjres/BILLSTATUS-119sjres193.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:30:24.690Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-sjres-193","plainSummary":"This joint resolution would require the President to withdraw U.S. military forces from any hostilities in or against Cuba unless Congress has formally declared war or authorized the use of force. The measure includes exceptions allowing the U.S. to defend itself from attack or imminent attack, and to continue lawful counternarcotics operations. It addresses the constitutional balance between executive and legislative authority over military deployments.","industries":[{"industry":"defense","confidence":0.7,"rationale":"The resolution would directly affect military deployment decisions and operational scope for defense contractors supporting U.S. fo